\chapter{Structured diagnostics}
\label{c:sdiag}\cutname{sdiag.html}

Both the bytecode and native compiler can emit diagnostic under
a structured format by using the "--log-format <format>" flag.
Supported formats are currently
\begin{itemize}
\item json
\item s-expression
\end{itemize}

\section{s:sdiag_versioning}{Versioning policy and subtyping}

In order to make the compiler diagnostics as easy to use as possible by
independent tools, The compiler and its tools enforce a strict versioning
and evolution policy.

This policy is a data-oriented version of semantic versioning for algebraic data
types:

\begin{itemize}
\item minor updates can only refine schemata into a subtype of the previous schema
\item major update can delete deprecated fields from record type or add new
  constructors to sum type.
\end{itemize}

Then a versioned and structured diagnostic can be seen as an algebraic data
types annotated with versioning and evolution annotation.

\begin{caml_example*}{verbatim}
type error_kind =
  | Fatal of string [@since 1.0][@deleted 1.1]
  | Error of string [@since 1.0]
  | Warning of
      { contents:string; number:int; name:string }
      [@since 1.0] [@expanded 1.1]
  | Lint of string [@preview 1.1][@since 2.0]
\end{caml_example*}


With this policy, we can ensure that a compiler at version "major.minor" can
output a structured diagnostic for any version "major.prev" where "prev" $\le$
"minor" by coercing the diagnostic at version "major.minor" into its subtype at
version "major.prev".


\subsection{ss:sdiag-record-subtyping}{Record subtyping rule}

The subtyping rules for records are straightforward because we can
easily elide fields when coercing to a previous version.
This gives us three subtyping rules:

\begin{enumerate}
\item add new fields to a record type
\item promote an optional field to a required field
\item deprecate a field
\end{enumerate}

In particular, the last two rules only refine metadata information about the
field and do not affect coercion.


\subsection{ss:sdiag-variant-subtyping}{Variant constructors subtyping rules}

The three subtyping rules for constructor are more interesting:

\begin{enumerate}
\item remove constructors from a sum type
\item expand the argument type of a variant constructor
\item introduce a new derived variant constructor
\end{enumerate}


The first rule is straightforward: if diagnostic consumers know how to handle
a constructor, it doesn't matter if the compiler actually emit this constructor or not.

Unfortunately, this first rule is also insufficient by itself to allow any
meaningful evolution of the sum type. A solution is to allow minor update to add
constructors that are related to previously existing constructors. There are two
relationship that seems worthy to track.

First, consider a constructor "c" which exists in both "v1" $<$ "v2" with only a
change of its argument type. If the argument type in the "v2" version is a
subtype of the argument type in "v1", then consumers can coerce the new type
to the old one.

Second, if the semantics of a new constructor "c2" in "v2" $>$ "v1" might be
approximated by an old constructor "c1" in "v1", then the compiler can
coerce the new constructor to the old one in version "v1".

For instance, one of the sum type presents in compiler diagnostics is the error
kind, which is represented as:

\begin{caml_example*}{verbatim}
type report_kind =
  | Report_error
  | Report_warning of string
  | Report_warning_as_error of string
  | Report_alert of string
  | Report_alert_as_error of string
\end{caml_example*}

It seems possible that in the future the compiler may want to
update this part of the diagnostic to

\begin{itemize}
\item expand the "Report_warning"
argument to split the warning number and name in two fields
\item delete the "Report_warning_as_error" constructor
\item add a new constructor "Report_Lint", derived from non-error warning.
\end{itemize}

\begin{caml_example*}{verbatim}
type report_kind =
  | Report_error
  | Report_warning of { contents:string; name:string; number:int}
  | Report_lint of string
  | Report_alert of string
  | Report_alert_as_error of string
\end{caml_example*}

With the three expansion rules above, this change can be done in a minor update
"v2".

\subsection{ss:sdiag-major-update}{Backward-compatibility beyond major versions}

Going back to major version, when publish a new major version of the
diagnostics, we may

\begin{enumerate}
\item delete unused records and sum types
\item remove a deprecated field from a record type
\item add a new variant constructor to a variant type
\end{enumerate}

With those changes, enforcing compatibility between major versions is not always
possible, in particular for the variant constructor side. However, we can afford
some long term guarantees.

First, to avoid reuse of previous names with different meaning, we must keep a
set of tombstones for deleted nominal types, record fields, and sum
constructors.

Second, in the record case, the specification of deleting only deprecated fields
means that we already keep producing deprecated fields for at least one compiler
version.


Third, backward-compatibility for variant constructor is more complex. If there
is a completely new constructor in version "major.0", it is not always possible
to translate this new constructor to a constructor in the previous version. We
could replace future constructor by a special constructor "<future>", but it is
unclear if this would help tools more than having an unknown constructor name.
Thus reliable deserializers that expect to work across major version would need
to parse variant sum as if they were always extensible.


\subsection{ss:sdiag-variant-subtyping}{Variant constructors and record fields lifecycle}

Resuming the previous evolution rules, this means that constructors and records goes
through stages during the evolution of a diagnostic

\begin{enumerate}
\item preview "[\@preview M.m]", a derived constructor can be created in a preview state
\item publication "[\@since M.m]"
\item expansion "[\@expanded M.m]", a constructor argument can be expanded
\item requirement "[\@required M.m]", an optional record field can be made mandatory
\item deprecation "[\@deprecated M.m]"
\item deletion "[\@deleted M.m]"
\end{enumerate}


\section{s:metadata-diag}{Compiler diagnostics}

The current schema of compiler diagnostics are hosted on in json-schema format
on \href{https://github.com/ocaml/ocaml}. Moreover, this section contains a more
human readable retranscription of those schema in the form of annotated
algebraic data types.

The current last version is:
\begin{itemize}
\item "1.0" for the "metadata" diagnostic
\item "1.0" for the "config" diagnostic
\item "1.0" for the compiler, toplevel and derived diagnostics
\end{itemize}

All those schema descriptions (and more) are generated using the
"ocamldiaginfo" tool available in the compiler source tree.

\subsection{ss:metadata-diag}{Metadata information}

The metadata field is present at the root of diagnostics directly
emitted by the compiler and contains both information about the version
of the scheme used to emit the diagnostics and if this specific diagnostic
is valid according to this scheme.

\input{sdiag_validity.tex}
\input{sdiag_metadata.tex}

